<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communist Party of Indonesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PKI, Partai Komunis Indonesia, Communist Party of the Indies</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> (PKI) grew out of the Indies Social Democratic Association founded in 1914 and took the name Communist Party of the Indies in 1920, one of the first communist parties in Asia. It was banned after its failed uprisings of 1926–1927. Revived after independence, it became the largest communist party outside the communist states before it was destroyed in the mass killings of 1965–1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia.jpg" alt="Communist Party of Indonesia" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Historyandideology, vectorised by Zt-freak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_of_the_Communist_Party_of_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, known as the PKI, was a Marxist-Leninist and Maoist party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and later Indonesia. Its forerunner, the Indies Social Democratic Association, was founded in 1914 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> socialist Henk Sneevliet with about 85 members. Its Dutch members introduced communist ideas to educated Indonesians looking for ways to oppose colonial rule. After Sneevliet moved its headquarters from Surabaya to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>, it attracted many Indonesians. At its congress of 23 May 1920 in Semarang the association changed its name to one that called it communist, with Semaun as chairman, and the PKI name was adopted in 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party planned a revolution against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1926, but a clampdown that year drove it underground, and its leaders disagreed about timing. A limited revolt began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> on 12 November, with similar ones at Padang, Bantam and Surabaya. The Batavia revolt was crushed within a day or two and the others within a few weeks, and the colonial government banned the party. It re-emerged after the Japanese surrender of 1945 and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a>.</p><p>Under D. N. Aidit, who became General Secretary in January 1951, the party grew from 3,000–5,000 members in 1950 to 1.5 million in 1959. It finished fourth in the 1955 election with 16.35 percent of the vote and 39 of 257 seats. It supported President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>'s Guided Democracy and in 1960 accepted his Nasakom formula of nationalism, religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. By 1965 it had about three million members and was supported by large labour, peasant, youth, women's and cultural organisations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On the night of 30 September 1965 six of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s top generals were killed by a group calling itself the 30 September Movement. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> put down the coup by 2 October, and the army blamed the PKI. The extent of the party's responsibility remains disputed: some leaders, Aidit among them, were involved, but evidence linking the party is inconclusive.</p><p>In the anti-communist purge that followed, an estimated 500,000 people or more were killed, with estimates running from 100,000 to two million, among them members, alleged sympathisers and people killed by mistake. A tribunal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> concluded in 2016 that the killings were crimes against humanity, and declassified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> cables released in 2017 show that the United States supplied aid and lists of names. The party's leadership was destroyed and the PKI was banned in 1966.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Communist Party of Indonesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Western-style schools created a small educated elite, and the first modern organisations followed: Budi Utomo in 1908, Sarekat Islam, which grew into a mass movement in the 1910s, the <b>Communist Party of the Indies</b> in 1920 and Sukarno’s Indonesian Nationalist Party in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The period of parliamentary democracy under the provisional constitution of 1950 produced short-lived coalition cabinets and, in 1955, the first free general election, in which the Nationalist Party, two Islamic parties and the Communist Party (<b>PKI</b>) each won a large share.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Augusto Pinochet’s rule in Chile used authoritarian means to secure investment, bringing growth and high inequality, and Suharto’s extirpation of the <b>Communist Party of Indonesia</b> allowed capitalism to expand there.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
